1
00:00:07,350 --> 00:00:13,348
In the first episode, propose and reject stopped after three days, but that was one example.

2
00:00:13,348 --> 00:00:17,159
The stopping rule waits for a day on which nobody is refused,

3
00:00:17,159 --> 00:00:20,722
so could there be lists for which such a day never comes?

4
00:00:20,883 --> 00:00:23,617
Look at what a day with a refusal does.

5
00:00:23,617 --> 00:00:30,515
In the evening the refused job crosses a name off its list, and a name that is crossed off never comes back.

6
00:00:30,515 --> 00:00:36,375
On day one Control crossed off Anita, and on day two it crossed off Bridget.

7
00:00:36,550 --> 00:00:41,346
So every day that does not stop the algorithm uses up at least one name.

8
00:00:41,346 --> 00:00:47,248
And the supply is limited, since three jobs with three names each make nine names in all.

9
00:00:47,248 --> 00:00:54,304
That means at most nine days can have a refusal, and after that a day with no refusal has to come.

10
00:00:54,483 --> 00:00:57,501
Nothing in this count was special about three.

11
00:00:57,501 --> 00:01:04,117
With any number of jobs, and as many candidates, the number of names is that number times itself.

12
00:01:04,117 --> 00:01:12,259
So only that many days can have a refusal, and propose and reject always halts, whatever the lists are.

13
00:01:14,216 --> 00:01:19,618
Halting is half of what we want, and the other half is that the result is stable.

14
00:01:19,618 --> 00:01:26,484
For that we need to know what a candidate holds from day to day, so let us replay the three days of Anita.

15
00:01:26,650 --> 00:01:33,300
On day one Approximation and Control both made her an offer, and she kept Approximation in hand.

16
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:39,566
On days two and three Approximation simply asked again, and she kept it again.

17
00:01:39,716 --> 00:01:44,974
Now pick any job that ever made Anita an offer, for example Control on day one.

18
00:01:44,974 --> 00:01:49,968
From that day on, what Anita holds is never below Control on her own list.

19
00:01:49,968 --> 00:01:55,576
The same is true for Approximation, where what she holds is exactly as good,

20
00:01:55,576 --> 00:01:58,158
because it is Approximation itself.

21
00:01:58,316 --> 00:02:00,338
Here is the claim in general.

22
00:02:00,338 --> 00:02:04,282
Suppose a job makes an offer to a candidate on some day.

23
00:02:04,282 --> 00:02:10,424
Then at the end of that day, and of every later day, she holds an offer she likes at least as much.

24
00:02:10,424 --> 00:02:15,614
This is called the improvement lemma, and one example is not a proof of it.

25
00:02:17,583 --> 00:02:23,385
Take a candidate and a job that makes her an offer, and look at the afternoon of that same day.

26
00:02:23,385 --> 00:02:29,531
She has at least this one offer to choose from, and the rule says she keeps the best of what she has.

27
00:02:29,531 --> 00:02:34,525
So at the end of that day she holds this job or one she likes more.

28
00:02:34,683 --> 00:02:39,577
Now suppose that at the end of some day she holds a job that is at least this good.

29
00:02:39,577 --> 00:02:43,247
The question is whether that job will ask her again tomorrow.

30
00:02:43,247 --> 00:02:47,438
She did not refuse it, and a job makes only one offer a day,

31
00:02:47,438 --> 00:02:50,931
so in the evening it crossed nothing off its list.

32
00:02:51,083 --> 00:02:56,549
So the next morning her name is still the first one on its list that is not crossed off,

33
00:02:56,549 --> 00:02:58,475
exactly as it was this morning.

34
00:02:58,475 --> 00:03:01,707
The morning rule then makes that job ask her again.

35
00:03:01,707 --> 00:03:04,594
That is the step the whole proof turns on,

36
00:03:04,594 --> 00:03:09,337
because an offer that was not refused always comes back the next day.

37
00:03:09,516 --> 00:03:16,362
So tomorrow afternoon she again has that job among her offers, and again she keeps the best of what she has.

38
00:03:16,362 --> 00:03:20,180
What she holds tomorrow is that job or one she likes more.

39
00:03:20,180 --> 00:03:27,450
And since that job was at least as good as the original one, what she holds tomorrow is at least as good too.

40
00:03:27,616 --> 00:03:34,066
So the claim is true on the day of the offer, and whenever it is true on one day it is true on the next.

41
00:03:34,066 --> 00:03:39,622
That carries it from day to day forever, which is a proof by induction on the days.

42
00:03:39,622 --> 00:03:43,600
The hand of a candidate can only climb her list.

43
00:03:43,783 --> 00:03:48,727
A job, meanwhile, starts at the top of its list and can only move down,

44
00:03:48,727 --> 00:03:52,209
because names are crossed off and never come back.

45
00:03:52,209 --> 00:03:56,929
So candidates climb while jobs sink, and somewhere the two have to meet.

46
00:03:56,929 --> 00:04:02,777
Whether the place where they meet is always stable is the question we are heading for.

